  "title": "Orchard Pollination Commons",
  "rules": [
    "Twelve four-person households provide exactly 48 controllable actors. The twelve pollinators are separate ecology entities with no action rows.",
    "The valley uses actual grass, hedges, plots, orchard gates, sluices and village stores. Work on the cell you face; turn actions do not move.",
    "Flowering plots need at least two pollination visits and enough growth age to fruit. Grafting advances their timing; pruning reduces disease or composts a spent plot.",
    "Every second non-winter tick, a placed apiary's bee follows its horizontal corridor within five cells of the hive. After relocation an out-of-range bee heads back toward the hive; gates and hedges can stop and reverse it.",
    "Apiarists can lift and carry a hive; its bee waits until it is placed on that hive's original lane. A relocated hive changes which flowering plots its bee can reach.",
    "An open sluice diverts passing water to its two plots. Closing it sends water downstream. Wet soil can spread disease through visiting bees; a new barrier directly hitting a bee reduces its real health.",
    "Harvest with a basket and bring fruit to a store. Every four ticks each row market chooses one nonempty queue using rotating priority; other queues wait.",
    "This warm orchard scene is bound to source state. No native play session, animation, action effect, solvability, training or transfer has been executed."
  ],
